data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider allowed synchronous LINQ operations via an opt-in (`CosmosOptionsExtension` / `EnableSyncOverAsync`). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** Synchronous query and save operations on the Cosmos provider now throw `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally — there is no workaround short of switching to async.

**All four methods break:**

| Sync call | Replacement |
|-----------|-------------|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

## Fixed Version

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- Remove any `EnableSyncOverAsync()` configuration — it no longer compiles.
- The change is Cosmos-specific; the relational providers still support sync APIs (for now).
- `Add()` remains synchronous (it's an in-memory operation on the change tracker), only the terminal query operators and `SaveChanges` are affected.
